<Fragment>
  <!--
    White paper, cool neutrals, one accent per part.

    The ink tone (14161C) sits next to the diagram background in
    proposal/diagrams, so a figure dropped on the page reads as part of the
    document rather than a pasted screenshot. Everything else is a single
    neutral ramp — a second warm or coloured family on white reads as dirt.

    `t-accent` (1B4A9C) is the document's inline accent and belongs to no
    part: a keyword, a figure number, an icon, a requirement badge, a table's
    answer column. It is spent once per block and never twice in one.

    The nine 부 colours are page *chrome* only — the masthead band, the
    running head's part numeral, the section bar, the divider and the contents
    row. Chrome says which part the reader is in; the inline accent says which
    word matters. Keeping them apart is what stops a page turning into two
    competing colour systems.

    They are not written here. `styles/parts.xml` holds them, generated from
    the one table in build.ts that also emits the masters — a palette written
    out twice drifts.

    There are no surface tokens here. The tinted surfaces this document
    allows — the running head's panel, a section bar, a table header row —
    carry their fill in the style that defines them, so a surface cannot be
    reached for by accident from a chapter file.
  -->
  <Styles>
    <Style name="t-ink"    color="14161C" />
    <Style name="t-body"   color="2E3138" />
    <Style name="t-muted"  color="6B7280" />
    <Style name="t-accent" color="1B4A9C" />
  </Styles>
</Fragment>
